 "result_text": "`Git.version_info` now refreshes after `git.refresh()` or `Git.refresh()`. In your example, `g1.version_info` would match `g1.version()` after you call `git.refresh(\"/usr/bin/git\")`.\n\nI only ran a quick check here, not your two-git setup and not the test suite. On this machine, `g.version_info` returned `(2, 47, 3)` both before and after `git.refresh()`. That confirms the property still works and that the cache is re-validated after a refresh, but it doesn't show a version actually changing.\n\nThe fix is in `git/cmd.py`. `Git.refresh` now sets a new marker on the class every time it runs, including when it reverts to the old executable after a failed refresh. Each instance records the marker it saw when it cached `version_info`. When the marker no longer matches, the property throws away the cached value and recomputes it.\n\nI also updated the `version_info` docstring to say the cache is invalidated on refresh. The new per-instance marker is excluded from pickling, the same way `_version_info` already was.",
